            {
               "text": "### Images from Mexico City Museo\n\nThese images are copyright for Mexico City Museo and allowed to be used according to the license determined by them.\n\nTo visualize all images, use the left and right arrows on the slide below.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htSlides\"><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">1 / 4</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgd700\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgd700', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Aztec Sun Stone on display at the National Museum of Anthropology in Mexico City (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>).<br />Photo taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the Museum.</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Mesoamerican calendars (First Steps (Volume 1))</li><li>Understanding the Multiplication Table (6 and 7) (First Steps (Volume 1))</li><li>History: Cradles of civilization (Part I) - While humanity originated in Africa, the first civilizations emerged in various regions around the world, such as Mesopotamia, the Andes, and Mesoamerica. (<htcite4>)</li><li>Who?: Who truly discovered America (Abya Yala)? (<htcite2>)</li><li>Mathematical Games: Building to count (Suanpan, 算盤) - In this text, we see the differences and what is in common between the Chinese abacus and the Japanese abacus. (<htcite5>)</li><li>Mathematical Games: Exercising with numbers - We do a physical exercise with the numbers to reinforce the addition of units and tens. (<htcite6>)</li><li>Mathematical Games: Multiplying with the body (Level 1) - In this text, we deepen our knowledge about the evolution of human beings, in addition to multiplying using our own bodies. (<htcite7>)</li><li>Mathematical Games: Walking and singing - Who was the first person in the family to walk? What is the relationship of this to indigenous numbers? (<htcite8>)</li><li>Physics: Together we are strong - In this text, we see how the four operations, when combined, open the doors to so much knowledge (<htcite9>)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">2 / 4</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgHs3\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgHs3', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Mammoth fossil exhibited in Mexico. Photograph taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the National Museum of Anthropology in Mexico ().</div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">3 / 4</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"mamut\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('mamut', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\"></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">4 / 4</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgOaxaca\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgOaxaca', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\"></div></div><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-left htSlidePrev\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(-1);\"></i><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right htSlideNext\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(1);\"></i></div></p>",
